# BlueRetroForeverRoot
Root repository to sync esp-idf and BlueRetroForever versions.

BlueRetroForever is an active fork of the archived [BlueRetro](https://github.com/darthcloud/BlueRetro)
by Jacques Gagnon — see that repo for the original project's documentation.

## Repository layout (read this first)

Three repositories nest here. Know which is which:

| Repo | What it is | You edit it? |
|---|---|---|
| **BlueRetroForever** (this submodule) | The ESP32 firmware source — the actual code. Default branch **`main`** carries the ongoing work (N64 keyboard/mouse, etc.). | **Yes — this is the code.** |
| **esp-idf** (this submodule) | Espressif's SDK + Xtensa toolchain. The firmware *cannot compile without it*. Pinned to a specific commit that matches the CI image. | No |
| **BlueRetroForeverRoot** (this repo) | A build-harness superproject. Its only job: pin one firmware commit + one matching esp-idf commit together (so a build is reproducible) and provide the `blueretro_dev.sh` environment + setup guides. | Only to bump the pinned versions |

Mental model: **root** = "which firmware + which SDK, wired together"; **BlueRetroForever** = the
source you change; **esp-idf** = the compiler. Develop in the `BlueRetroForever/` submodule (on
`main`); bump this root's submodule pointer when you want to publish a new known-good pairing:

```bash
git submodule update --remote BlueRetroForever   # advance the firmware pointer to origin/main
git add BlueRetroForever && git commit -m "BlueRetro ref <version>"
```

# Development instructions

[Instructions for Ubuntu 20.04+](#blueretro-development-on-ubuntu-2004)\
[Instructions for macOS 13+](README.macos)\
[Instructions for Windows 10+](README.windows)

These instructions likely mostly apply for older version and/or different distribution as well.
But they are not tested, so YMMV and you're on your own.

# BlueRetro development on Ubuntu 20.04+

## 1 - Initial setup

### 1.1 - Install ESP-IDF Prerequisites
1. Open a Terminal (like gnome-terminal or konsole).
2. Do the following command:
   ```bash
   sudo apt-get install git wget flex bison gperf python3 python3-pip python3-venv cmake ninja-build ccache libffi-dev libssl-dev dfu-util libusb-1.0-0
   ```

### 1.2 - BlueRetro code checkout
1. Open a Terminal (like gnome-terminal or konsole).
3. Do the following commands:
   ```bash
   cd ~
   git clone https://github.com/bherdm/BlueRetroForeverRoot.git
   cd ~/BlueRetroForeverRoot/
   git submodule update --init --recursive
   export IDF_TOOLS_PATH=$(pwd)
   cd esp-idf
   ./install.sh
   ```

##  2 - Compiling BlueRetro firmware
1. Open a Terminal (like gnome-terminal or konsole).
3. Do the following commands:
   ```bash
   cd ~/BlueRetroRoot/
   . ./blueretro_dev.sh
   cd BlueRetroForever
   # Replace configs/hw1/universal by any other config you may wish
   cp configs/hw1/universal sdkconfig
   idf.py build
   # Replace serial device with your own
   idf.py -p /dev/ttyUSB0 flash
   idf.py -p /dev/ttyUSB0 monitor
   ```

# ESP32 embedded development tips

## Dump spiffs
```bash
esptool.py --chip esp32 read_flash 0x310000 0xF0000 spiffs.bin
mkspiffs.exe -u spiffs_unpack -b 4096 -p 256 -s 0xF0000 .\spiffs.bin
```

## Delete spiffs
```bash
esptool.py erase_region 0x310000 0xF0000
```

## QEMU
```bash
(cd build; esptool.py --chip esp32 merge_bin --fill-flash-size 4MB -o flash_image.bin @flash_args)
```
```bash
qemu-system-xtensa -machine esp32 -drive file=build/flash_image.bin,if=mtd,format=raw -serial stdio -display none -nic user,model=open_eth,id=lo0,hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:8001-:80
```

## Running GitHub action offline

Using [github.com/nektos/act](https://github.com/nektos/act)

Checkout main repo separately, then:
```bash
act -W .github/workflows/quick.yml --input hw_version=hw1 --input br_config=universal --artifact-server-path /tmp
```

